# End-of-task commit recommendation

When you finish a user request and all related work is done, always end your response with a **Recommended commit** section unless the user explicitly says not to.

## What to include

1. **Suggested commit title** — conventional commit format (`feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `chore`, etc.)
2. **Optional body** — 1–3 lines explaining *why*, not a file list
3. **Files to stage** — short list of paths to `git add` (only changed files for that task)
4. **Ready-to-run command** — full `git add` + `git commit` block the user can copy

## Do not commit automatically

- Never run `git commit` unless the user explicitly asks you to commit.
- Only recommend the message and commands.

## Message style

- Title: imperative mood, ≤72 characters, no trailing period
- Body: complete sentences; focus on purpose and impact
- Match repo tone when recent commits use conventional commits

## Example closing section

```markdown
## Recommended commit

**Title:** `feat(ui): add LMS wordmark branding to client and admin apps`

**Body:**
Unify navigation and auth screens with a shared Logo component and Syne display font for consistent branding.

\`\`\`bash
git add lms-client/src/components/Logo.jsx lms-admin/src/components/Logo.jsx ...
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
feat(ui): add LMS wordmark branding to client and admin apps

Unify navigation and auth screens with a shared Logo component and Syne display font for consistent branding.
EOF
)"
\`\`\`
```
